/** \page toolbarpersistence Toolbar identity and layout persistence

FreeCAD stores toolbar layouts independently for each workbench and, where
needed, for a context within a workbench. This page describes the identity and
state model used by Gui::ToolBarManager and by workbench implementations.

\section toolbarpersistence_identity Toolbar identity

Every toolbar can have a persistence identity consisting of a scope, a stable
toolbar identifier, and the identifiers required by that scope. The stable
identifier is distinct from the toolbar's label: labels are user-facing and may
be translated or changed, while persistence identifiers must remain
untranslated and stable across releases.

Gui::ToolBarManager serializes typed identities into persistence keys. The
serialized form is an implementation detail and its components are escaped so
arbitrary Unicode identifiers and delimiter characters round-trip safely.
Callers should construct and inspect identities through ToolBarManager rather
than assemble or parse persistence keys themselves.

\section toolbarpersistence_scopes Scopes

Toolbar identities use one of four scopes:

- A shared toolbar represents the same toolbar across workbenches.
- A workbench toolbar belongs to one workbench and has layout state in that
  workbench's layout.
- A contextual toolbar belongs to a named context within a workbench, such as
  Sketcher edit mode.
- A legacy identity has only a toolbar name and exists to read settings written
  before scoped identities were introduced.

A workbench layout is the base layout state. Activating a contextual layout
selects a separate layout for that context without overwriting the base
workbench arrangement. Leaving the context restores the workbench layout.
Shared and legacy toolbars keep one placement and ordering across workbenches;
workbench and contextual layouts only arrange toolbars owned by that scope.
Changing a shared toolbar's position therefore changes it for every workbench,
while resetting a workbench or context leaves shared toolbar placement alone.

\section toolbarpersistence_state Layout state

A saved layout records toolbar area, ordering, and row breaks. With
workbench-aware persistence enabled, shared and legacy toolbar placement is
stored once under <tt>BaseApp/MainWindow/SharedToolBarLayout</tt>, while
workbench and contextual layouts are stored as structured parameter groups
under <tt>BaseApp/MainWindow/WorkbenchLayouts</tt>. Toolbar visibility remains
a user preference, separate from placement. User preferences controlling the
feature remain under <tt>BaseApp/Preferences/MainWindow</tt>; these are separate
parameter trees with different ownership.

The active scoped layout is restored after workbench activation. This ordering
allows the workbench and its modules to create their toolbars first, then
applies persisted placement and visibility without module initialization
overwriting the user's arrangement.

Toolbar visibility also has a declared default: visible, hidden, or
unavailable. Unavailable toolbars are omitted from the normal visibility UI for
that state. Toolbar tiers classify available toolbars as recommended,
secondary, advanced, or contextual; they provide presentation and reset
defaults but do not replace the toolbar's persistence identity.

\section toolbarpersistence_python Python workbenches

Python workbenches provide the same metadata through ToolbarOptions when
calling PythonWorkbench::appendToolbar(). For example:

\code{.py}
self.appendToolbar(
    "Measurement tools",
    ("My_MeasureLength", "My_MeasureArea"),
    FreeCADGui.ToolbarOptions(
        id="MeasurementTools",
        scope=FreeCADGui.ToolbarScopeId.workbench("MyWorkbench"),
        tier=FreeCADGui.ToolbarTier.Secondary,
        visibility=FreeCADGui.ToolbarVisibility.Visible,
    ),
)
\endcode

Here, <tt>Measurement tools</tt> is the user-facing label and
<tt>MeasurementTools</tt> is the stable identifier. Changing the stable
identifier or its scope creates a different persistence identity, so existing
saved state for the old identity will no longer apply.

*/
